<!--
  The sibling member, filed in Portugal. Read it with merge-us-member.xml, which lists the
  cases this pair arranges.

  What to read closely here:

    * `pessoal` is the sibling's `labour`, and the contributed facts agree exactly: the
      same `interference`, the same `capacity` absorber cited from the same URI, the same
      `people` among its holders. Every one of those compares with no negotiation at
      all. The names do not, and neither do the units.

    * Its remainder is partly visible, where the sibling's is not. This entity operates
      an hours bank, so part of the excess its people absorb becomes an obligation it
      must carry: a `booked` holder with a figure in it, beside the `people` holder.
      Same layer, same fit, same absorber, different holders, and the difference is
      jurisdictional rather than managerial. Neither filing is more honest than the
      other.

    * `compute` shares only a word with the sibling's. A different vendor sells a
      monthly reservation metered in GPU-hours. Nothing in either document says these
      are two layers, and nothing says they are one either.

    * The absorber on `capacidade-instalada` cites a translated edition of the buffer
      vocabulary, the one its internal reporting is written against. `capacidade` and
      `capacity` are the same buffer. A bare code would make them look like a
      disagreement; the taxonomy makes them a fork, visible, and resolvable by whoever
      owns the two editions.

  Illustrative: the taxonomy URIs and figures are placeholders, and the hours bank is
  sketched only as far as this model needs it.
-->
<pm:processModulus xmlns:pm="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0">

  <!-- Which filing this is: the URN a part or a dependence cites to point at this
